# EVERY REFERENCE INTO THE LAYER DIMENSION, WITH WHAT THE REFERRING KEY ADDS TO IT. One row per
# foreign key into `pm.layer`, and `whole` where it names the layer entire.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THIS IS WHAT A PARTITION ACTUALLY LOOKS LIKE, AND IT IS THE PRECONDITION OF "A LAYER IS A
#    LANE". `(filing, layer)` is a partition cell because it is the COARSEST KEY THAT NOTHING
#    REFINES: every table mentioning a layer either EXTENDS it with a second dimension
#    (`slack` + buffer, `holder` + kind, `draw`/`induction` + operation) or RELATES two whole
#    layers (`coupling`, `part`). Not one of them names a PIECE of a layer. If any did, a lane
#    would have to be that finer thing and the whole mapping would move down a level.
#
# ⭐⭐ AND IT IS WHY EMITTING ONE LANE SET IS A SOUND ELIMINATION RATHER THAN A SIMPLIFICATION.
#    D and N are two fact tables on ONE conformed dimension, so their lane sets are two
#    enumerations of the same 51 cells. `|D lanes ∪ N lanes| = |D| + |N| - e` with `e` the cells
#    themselves, which is the fold with the overlap DERIVED rather than filed: the overlap
#    of two lane sets IS the layer set, and the layer set is a relation with a name.
#
# ⛔ THE ENGINES DO NOT SUBDIVIDE THE PARTITION, AND THAT IS THE THIRD ELIMINATION, ALREADY MADE
#   BY THE MODEL. `pm.draw` has no `buffer` column: a draw is against a LAYER, never against one
#   of its three engines, because `layers/absorption.sqlc` states they are SUBSTITUTES. So D is
#   P x L and not P x L x 3, and the buffer attribution nobody can make is eliminated before any
#   diagram exists. ⚠️ What the engines DO explain is why a lane is EMPTY: `pm.slack` holds three
#   rows per layer and every one of them renders as `none`.
# ⛔⛔ AND IT SEES THE SCHEMA ONLY, WHICH IS WORTH KNOWING BECAUSE THE KEY CAN BE LOST OUTSIDE IT.
#    This reads `pg_constraint`, so it can prove every foreign key into `pm.layer` names both
#    columns and can prove nothing about a RELATION that flattens them. Two places have done it:
#    `rank/graph_edges.sqlc` concatenates on purpose, because everything downstream of it counts
#    and a count needs only distinct ids; and three rules put a claim address in the checker's
#    own `layer` column, which `checks/grain.sqlc`'s `unresolvable` arm now refuses. ⭐ Two
#    guards, one property: this one holds it in the schema, that one holds it in the output.
-- pg_constraint, for the foreign keys into pm.layer; the grain the lane mapping rests on.
SELECT c.conrelid::regclass::text                       AS referrer,
       pg_get_constraintdef(c.oid)                      AS reference,
       cardinality(c.confkey) = 2                       AS whole
FROM pg_constraint c
WHERE c.contype = 'f' AND c.confrelid = 'pm.layer'::regclass
